# Patch: Train the MoE router from bandit feedback (REINFORCE) instead of the task gradient
#
# Problem:
#   The MoE router normally learns from the task gradient that flows back through
#   the mixing weights it produces. moe-shaping asks what happens if the router
#   instead only receives *bandit* feedback: it picks experts, the model runs, and
#   the resulting loss is the only thing it is told. src/bandit.py does this for
#   the toy MoE; this patch does it for a real LM in Megatron.
#
#   The awkward part at LM scale is credit assignment. The toy model has one
#   router and per-expert losses are directly observable (semi-bandit feedback).
#   An LM has one router per layer and a single loss at the output, so the reward
#   for a routing decision is only observable through the final per-token loss,
#   shared by every layer the token passed through.
#
# What this patch does:
#   1. megatron/core/transformer/moe/bandit.py (new): the whole mechanism.
#      - BanditConfig, populated once from the --bandit-* args.
#      - record(): per-micro-batch store of each layer's routing action (the
#        per-token log-probability of the selected top-k set, its entropy, and
#        optionally the critic's prediction).
#      - build_loss(): given the per-token LM loss, builds the REINFORCE
#        surrogate  -sum_layers advantage_t * log pi(a_t | s_t)  plus the optional
#        entropy bonus and critic regression, and returns it as a scalar to add to
#        the *summed* task loss, so the pipeline schedule's division by token count
#        and micro-batch count normalizes both terms identically.
#      - BlockTaskGradient: identity with a zero backward. Used instead of
#        .detach() on the mixing logits so the router logits stay connected in the
#        autograd graph -- MoEAuxLossAutoScaler attaches the aux load-balancing
#        loss to the *probs* tensor and is silently skipped if that tensor does not
#        require grad -- while the task loss still contributes nothing to the
#        router weight.
#      - pop_metrics(): "bandit/*" diagnostics. All accumulation stays on the GPU
#        and is read back once per logging call, so the training step never blocks
#        on a synchronization for logging.
#   2. megatron/core/transformer/moe/router.py: TopKRouter.bandit_routing(), the
#      selection path (Gumbel-top-k or epsilon-greedy exploration, annealed over
#      the run), which records the action and blocks the task gradient. __init__
#      rejects the routing variants this path does not implement (sigmoid scoring,
#      pre-softmax, expert bias, token dropping, group-limited routing) rather than
#      ignoring them, and builds the per-layer critic head when that baseline is
#      selected. forward() stashes the flattened router input for the critic.
#      Also adds "router/entropy" to the MoE tracker for *every* arm (not just
#      bandit ones), so router collapse is visible next to the load statistics.
#   3. megatron/training/arguments.py (moe arg group): the --bandit-* flags.
#   4. megatron/training/training.py: bandit.configure(args) in pretrain() before
#      the model is built (the router inspects the config at construction time);
#      the exploration anneal advanced in train_step(); "bandit/*" logged in
#      training_log().
#   5. pretrain_gpt.py, loss_func(): hands the per-token loss to build_loss() and
#      adds the result to the loss.
#
# Reward and credit assignment:
#   reward_t = -(LM loss for token t), credited in full to that token's routing
#   decision in *every* MoE layer. The advantage is reward minus a baseline
#   (micro-batch mean, or a per-layer linear critic reading the router's own input,
#   which is the variance-reducing choice since most of the spread in token loss is
#   token difficulty rather than routing quality), optionally rescaled to unit
#   standard deviation.
#
# Token ordering:
#   The router flattens [seq, batch, hidden] -> [seq * batch]; the LM loss arrives
#   as [batch, seq]. build_loss() transposes before flattening so token t means the
#   same token on both sides. Getting this wrong silently trains on a permuted
#   reward, so keep the transpose if either shape convention changes.
#
# Diagnostics:
#   --bandit-grad-align-interval N logs "bandit/grad_alignment", the cosine
#   similarity between the task gradient and the REINFORCE gradient w.r.t. the
#   router logits -- i.e. how much of the true routing gradient the bandit
#   estimator actually recovers. It needs a task path to the logits, so it requires
#   --bandit-keep-task-gradient, and it costs two extra backward passes on the
#   iterations it fires.
#
# Scope / caveats:
#   * "bandit/*" metrics are the logging rank's local view (no cross-rank reduce),
#     matching the caveat on patches 0003/0004.
#   * The critic head lives inside the router module, so '*.router.*' param-group
#     overrides from patch 0002 (e.g. --router-lr) apply to it too.
#   * Only the plain softmax top-k routing path is implemented; see the asserts.
#
# How to find the right location in a future Megatron version:
#   router.py: TopKRouter.routing(), the `if self.routing_type == "sinkhorn"`
#     dispatch; and TopKRouter.forward() just before the self.routing() call.
#   training.py: `args = get_args()` at the top of pretrain(); the top of
#     train_step(); the 'samples vs steps' line in training_log().
#   pretrain_gpt.py: the `losses = output_tensor.view(-1).float()` block in
#     loss_func().
#   arguments.py: the 'moe' argument group, next to --router-lr from patch 0002.
#
# Applied to: Megatron-LM core_v0.16.1 (commit 55ac7082), on top of patches 0001-0005.
#
